# Issue #3135 (advisory op spans, PR 3): a rotation baseline may carry the
# optional, writer-signed `coversThroughUpdateId`, and the rotation-checkpoint
# refinement now also rejects that field on anything but a rotation baseline.
# No existing client sends the field, so no existing request is rejected.
# Every request that embeds a document sync update reports the reworded
# request refinement. Remove these exact diagnostics, and this file, as soon
# as main contains the new contract.
POST /auth/register: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /blobs/{blobId}/attachment-bindings: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /containers/with-metadata-document: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /documents/{documentId}/link: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /documents/{documentId}/sync: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /documents/{documentId}/unlink: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /documents: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
POST /organizations: refinement 'request.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.

# Issue #3135 PR 3: a served rotation baseline may carry the signed cursor,
# and the response refinement rejects it on anything else, which tightens what
# the server may send. A client built before this contract recomputes a
# cursor-signed baseline's metadata hash without the cursor and fails closed
# on it: a flag day for the greenfield SDK, which ships with the API. Remove
# this exact diagnostic as soon as main contains the new contract.
POST /documents/{documentId}/sync: refinement 'response.rotation-checkpoint-fields' was reworded; treat semantic changes as breaking.
